---
title: 'Time Axis'
description: 'Use $framework chart time axis to display time-based data in a $framework chart or $framework graph.'
---

Time axes are used to display time-based data in a chart. They can be used to show data at different levels of granularity, such as years, months, days, or even hours and minutes.

There are three methods of displaying time along an axis.

-   [Continuous Time Axis](#continuous-time) - Data is shown on a continuous scale.
-   [Unit Time Axis](#unit-time) - Each 'unit' has a dedicated band within the domain.
-   [Ordinal Time Axis](#ordinal-time) - Only provided values are shown, with missing values omitted.

The difference between a continuous [Continuous Time Axis](#continuous-time), a [Unit Time Axis](#unit-time), and an [Ordinal Time Axis](#ordinal-time) is demonstrated in the following example:

{% chartExampleRunner title="Time Axis Types" name="time-vs-unit-time-vs-ordinal-time" type="generated" /%}

## Continuous Time

The time axis is similar to the number axis in the sense that it is also used to plot continuous values. The time axis
can even be used with numeric data (in addition to `Date` objects), but the numbers will be interpreted as JavaScript
timestamps. The time axis differs from the number axis in tick segmentation and label formatting. For example, you could
choose to place a tick every 5 minutes, every month, or every Friday.

The time axis also supports specifier strings to control the way time values are presented as labels. For example,
the `%H:%M:%S` specifier string will instruct the axis to format a time value like `new Date('Mon Apr 17 2023 12:43:17')`
or `1681735397000` as `'12:43:17'`. Time axes are typically used as x-axes and placed at the bottom of a chart. The
simplest time axis config looks like this:

```js
{
    type: 'time',
    position: 'bottom',
}
```

For a full list of configuration options see [Continuous Time Axis Options](#reference-AgTimeAxisOptions).

## Unit Time

The Time Axis defaults to using a continuous scale. This accurately portrays the difference between each time value, but this is not always desired.
For example, data points will not be evenly spaced when plotting monthly data, because of the inconsistent number of days across months.

The Unit Time Axis will plot time values with evenly spaced bands for each unit of time, regardless of the actual time span between them.

```js
{
    type: 'unit-time',
    position: 'bottom',
}
```

The Unit Time Axis assumes data is evenly spaced, and will infer a time interval based on the data provided. If required, you can specify the `unit` to use for the axis. For example, if you want to display data in months, you can set the `unit` property to `'month'`:

```js
{
    type: 'unit-time',
    position: 'bottom',
    unit: 'month',
}
```

In this example of `unit: 'month'`, any units smaller than 'month' will be ignored. This means that values for 01 Jan and 28 Feb will be displayed identically to values of 31 Jan and 01 Feb.

See [Time Interval](#reference-AgTimeInterval) for more information on the `unit` property.

{% note %}

The Unit Time Axis does not aggregate data, so you should ensure that each series only has one value per `unit`.

{% /note %}

For a full list of configuration options see [Unit Time Axis Options](#reference-AgUnitTimeAxisOptions).

## Ordinal Time

The Ordinal Time Axis is used to plot time-bound data on a band scale. Unlike a linear [Continuous Time Axis](#continuous-time), which represents time with consistent intervals, the ordinal time scale maintains the order of time values but does not represent the exact time span between them.

Data points plotted along an Ordinal Time Axis will be arranged according to their position in time, ignoring the time intervals between them.

For example, if the Ordinal Time Axis is used to plot daily values but there are no data points for the weekend, values for Friday and Monday will be equally spaced, without gaps for Saturday and Sunday.

The Ordinal Time Axis can be used with `Date` objects and numeric data, the numbers will be interpreted as JavaScript timestamps.

This axis follows the tick segmentation and label formatting of the [Continuous Time Axis](#continuous-time).

Ordinal Time axes are commonly used for financial data on the x-axis, usually placed at the bottom of a chart. A basic Ordinal Time Axis configuration looks like this:

```js
{
    type: 'ordinal-time',
    position: 'bottom',
}
```

For a full list of configuration options see [Ordinal Time Axis Options](#reference-AgOrdinalTimeAxisOptions).

## Intervals

The interval of Time Axes can be customised with the `interval.step` parameter, which should be a
`AgTimeInterval` or `AgTimeIntervalUnit`.

{% chartExampleRunner title="Time Axis Interval" name="time-interval" type="generated" /%}

```js
{
    interval: {
        step: { unit: 'day', step: 7, new Date('2025-01-01') },
    },
}
```

```js
{
    interval: {
        step: 'month',
    },
}
```

In this configuration:

-   `unit` must be one of `millisecond`, `second`, `minute`, `hour`, `day`, `week`, `month`, or `year`.
-   `step` is an optional number that specifies the multiple of the unit to use.
-   `epoch` is an optional `Date` that specifies the starting point for the interval.

If the `step` property of a time axis is set to a `number`, this will be interpreted as milliseconds.

See [Time Interval](#reference-AgTimeInterval) for more information on the `step` property.

## Parent Levels

The `parentLevel` option allows displaying labels and ticks for the parent level of the time axes. For example, if the axis is showing monthly data, the parent level would be the years.
These levels are based on the data displayed, and change as the user zooms in and out.

It is enabled by default for the [Unit Time Axis](#unit-time), and can be opted into for the
[Continuous Time Axis](#continuous-time) and [Ordinal Time Axis](#ordinal-time).

{% chartExampleRunner title="Parent Level" name="axis-parent-level" type="generated" /%}

```js
{
    parentLevel: {
        enabled: true,
    },
}
```

In this example:

-   The data is shown with a 'day' unit, and the parent level of 'month' is shown in bold.
-   As you zoom out, the labels will change to 'month', with a parent level of 'year'.

### Customisation

Parent level labels and ticks options are inherited from the axes label and tick options, but can be customised with the `parentLevel` options.

{% chartExampleRunner title="Parent Level Customisation" name="axis-parent-level-customisation" type="generated" /%}

```js
{
    tick: {
        width: 0,
    },
    label: {
        format: {
            day: '%e',
            month: '%b',
        },
    },
    parentLevel: {
        enabled: true,
        tick: {
            width: 1,
        },
        label: {
            format: {
                month: '%e\n%b',
                year: '%b\n%Y',
            },
        },
    },
}
```

In this configuration:

-   The label format is set to show the day and month for the `day` level, and the month for the `month` level.
-   The parent level tick is given a width of `1` to show the parent level ticks.
-   The parent level label format is set to show the day and month on separate lines for the `month` level, and the month and year on separate lines for the `year` level.

See [Axis Labels](./axes-labels#label-text-formatting) and the [Formatters](./formatters#format) pages for more information on formatting labels.

